 useQuery
 
 =>When a component using useQuery renders, React Query immediately checks its cache. If cached data exists and is still fresh (within staleTime), it returns that data instantly without any API call. If cached data exists but is stale, it returns the cached data immediately for a fast UI, but also triggers a background refetch to update the data. If no cached data exists, it triggers an API call and shows a loading state. This makes useQuery excellent for server state management because it provides instant cached data while ensuring eventual consistency with the server.
 
Summary
=>useQuery runs during render, not after like useEffect
=>It provides instant cached data when available
=>Stale data doesn't automatically block rendering - it shows cached data and refetches in background
=>It's designed for optimistic UI - fast displays with background updates
=>The "server state" name comes from its ability to sync client cache with server data automatically

Also useQuery works the same when screen is focused && internet reconnectivity.

U can also control/stop all these behaviours by below keys:
refetchOnMount, refetchOnWindowFocus and refetchOnReconnect

